created with a few errors. See error description below.":"{{groupName}} user group created with a few errors. See error description below.","{{groupName}} user group created.":"{{groupName}} user group created.","{{hours}} hour":"{{hours}} hour","{{hours}} hour ago":"{{hours}} hour ago","{{hours}} hours":"{{hours}} hours","{{hours}} hours ago":"{{hours}} hours ago","{{iamUserName}}_credentials.csv":"{{iamUserName}}_credentials.csv","{{identityCenter}} instance still connected":"{{identityCenter}} instance still connected","{{label}} - Opens in new tab":"{{label}} - Opens in new tab","{{minutes}} minute":"{{minutes}} minute","{{minutes}} minute ago":"{{minutes}} minute ago","{{minutes}} minutes":"{{minutes}} minutes","{{minutes}} minutes ago":"{{minutes}} minutes ago","{{months}} month":"{{months}} month","{{months}} month ago":"{{months}} month ago","{{months}} months":"{{months}} months","{{months}} months ago":"{{months}} months ago","{{name}} is still connected to {{identityCenter}} instance {{instanceId}} from an earlier configuration. To keep this connection, select the instance again. Otherwise, it's removed when you choose Configure.":"{{name}} is still connected to {{identityCenter}} instance {{instanceId}} from an earlier configuration. To keep this connection, select the instance again. Otherwise, it's removed when you choose Configure.","{{name}} is still connected to {{identityCenter}} instance {{instanceId}}, but the connection isn't used while trusted identity propagation isn't configured.":"{{name}} is still connected to {{identityCenter}} instance {{instanceId}}, but the connection isn't used while trusted identity propagation isn't configured.","{{num}} actions":"{{num}} actions","{{num}} audiences":"{{num}} audiences","{{num}} days_one":"{{num}} day","{{num}} days_other":"{{num}} days","{{num}} hours_one":"{{num}} hour","{{num}} hours_other":"{{num}} hours","{{num}} inline policies added":"{{num}} inline policies added","{{num}} inline policies removed":"{{num}} inline policies removed","{{num}} minutes_one":"{{num}} minute","{{num}} minutes_other":"{{num}} minutes","{{num}} policies added to {{userName}}":"{{num}} policies added to {{userName}}","{{num}} policies removed":"{{num}} policies removed","{{num}} seconds_one":"{{num}} second","{{num}} seconds_other":"{{num}} seconds","{{num}} unused actions":"{{num}} unused actions","{{number}} available":"{{number}} available","{{number}} inline policies":"{{number}} inline policies","{{number}} rows":"{{number}} rows","{{policyName}} copied":"{{policyName}} copied","{{PrimaryButtonText}}":"{{PrimaryButtonText}}","{{resource}} Copied":"{{resource}} Copied","{{resourceLevelActionName}} action in {{actionLevelServiceName}}":"{{resourceLevelActionName}} action in {{actionLevelServiceName}}","{{resourceName}} copied":"{{resourceName}} copied","{{resourceName}} loaded":"{{resourceName}} loaded","{{resourceName}} loading":"{{resourceName}} loading","{{resourceType}} ARN":"{{resourceType}} ARN","{{roleName}} not deleted.":"{{roleName}} not deleted.","{{SecondaryButtonText}}":"{{SecondaryButtonText}}","{{service}} ({{num}} unused actions)":"{{service}} ({{num}} unused actions)","{{service}} (1 unused action)":"{{service}} (1 unused action)","{{service}} (all actions unused)":"{{service}} (all actions unused)","{{title}}":"{{title}}","{{userName}} user created with a few errors. See error description below.":"{{userName}} user created with a few errors. See error description below.","{{userName}}_credentials.csv":"{{userName}}_credentials.csv","{{weeks}} week":"{{weeks}} week","{{weeks}} week ago":"{{weeks}} week ago","{{weeks}} weeks":"{{weeks}} weeks","{{weeks}} weeks ago":"{{weeks}} weeks ago","{{years}} year":"{{years}} year","{{years}} year ago":"{{years}} year ago","{{years}} years":"{{years}} years","{{years}} years ago":"{{years}} years ago","< Actions":"< Actions","< Services":"< Services","<inputComponent/> characters":"<inputComponent/> characters","<p>A role is an IAM identity that you can create in your account that has specific permissions. An IAM role has some similarities to an IAM user. Roles and users are both AWS identities with permissions policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS. However, instead of being uniquely associated with one person, a role can be assumed by anyone who needs it. A role does not have standard long-term credentials such as a password or access keys associated with it. Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.</p><p>You can use roles to delegate access to users, applications, or services that don't normally have access to your AWS resources.</p>":"<p>A role is an IAM identity that you can create in your account that has specific permissions. An IAM role has some similarities to an IAM user. Roles and users are both AWS identities with permissions policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS. However, instead of being uniquely associated with one person, a role can be assumed by anyone who needs it. A role does not have standard long-term credentials such as a password or access keys associated with it. Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session.</p><p>You can use roles to delegate access to users, applications, or services that don't normally have access to your AWS resources.</p>",'<p>A user group is a collection of IAM users. User groups simplify permissions management by letting you grant, change, and remove permissions for multiple users at once. For example, you can create a user group named "Admins" and give that group administrative permissions. Any user in that group automatically has the permissions that are assigned to the group.</p>':'<p>A user group is a collection of IAM users. User groups simplify permissions management by letting you grant, change, and remove permissions for multiple users at once. For example, you can create a user group named "Admins" and give that group administrative permissions. Any user in that group automatically has the permissions that are assigned to the group.</p>',"<p>An AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) user is an entity that you create in AWS to represent the person or application that uses it to interact with AWS. A user in AWS consists of a name and credentials. </p>":"<p>An AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) user is an entity that you create in AWS to represent the person or application that uses it to interact with AWS. A user in AWS consists of a name and credentials. </p>","<p>IAM identity providers (IdPs) manage user identities outside of AWS. You can establish a trust relationship with an IdP to give external user identities permissions to use AWS resources in your account. That way, you don’t have to create and manage IAM users yourself. IdPs do these tasks for you</p><p>Using an IdP might be useful if your organization already has its own identity system, such as a corporate user directory. Also, if you are creating a mobile app or web application that requires access to AWS resources, using an IdP can help keep your AWS account secure. You don’t have to distribute or embed long-term security credentials, such as access keys, in your applications.</p><p>To use an IdP, create an IAM identity provider entity to establish a trust relationship between your AWS account and the IdP. IAM supports IdPs that are compatible with <openid>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</openid> or <samlLink>SAML 2.0 (Security Assertion Markup Language 2.0)</samlLink>.</p>":"<p>IAM identity providers (IdPs) manage user identities outside of AWS. You can establish a trust relationship with an IdP to give external user identities permissions to use AWS resources in your account. That way, you don’t have to create and manage IAM users yourself. IdPs do these tasks for you</p><p>Using an IdP might be useful if your organization already has its own identity system, such as a corporate user directory. Also, if you are creating a mobile app or web application that requires access to AWS resources, using an IdP can help keep your AWS account secure. You don’t have to distribute or embed long-term security credentials, such as access keys, in your applications.</p><p>To use an IdP, create an IAM identity provider entity to establish a trust relationship between your AWS account and the IdP. IAM supports IdPs that are compatible with <openid>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</openid> or <samlLink>SAML 2.0 (Security Assertion Markup Language 2.0)</samlLink>.</p>","<p>If you have employees that require access to AWS, don't share root user credentials with them. Instead, create individual IAM users within your account that correspond to users in your organization. IAM users are not separate accounts; they are users within the same account. Each user can have its own unique set of long-term credentials for access to the AWS Management Console. You can also create an individual access key for each user so that the user can make programmatic requests to work with resources in your account. You can grant different permissions to each IAM user.</p><p>Alternatively, you can <sso>use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication</sso>.</p>":"<p>If you have employees that require access to AWS, don't share root user credentials with them. Instead, create individual IAM users within your account that correspond to users in your organization. IAM users are not separate accounts; they are users within the same account. Each user can have its own unique set of long-term credentials for access to the AWS Management Console. You can also create an individual access key for each user so that the user can make programmatic requests to work with resources in your account. You can grant different permissions to each IAM user.</p><p>Alternatively, you can <sso>use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication</sso>.</p>","<p>Policies are JSON documents in AWS that let you specify who has access to AWS resources, and what actions they can perform on those resources. You can attach a policy to an identity or resource to define their permissions. AWS evaluates these policies when the IAM principal (user or role) makes a request. Permissions in the policies determine whether the request is allowed or denied.</p>":"<p>Policies are JSON documents in AWS that let you specify who has access to AWS resources, and what actions they can perform on those resources. You can attach a policy to an identity or resource to define their permissions. AWS evaluates these policies when the IAM principal (user or role) makes a request. Permissions in the policies determine whether the request is allowed or denied.</p>","<p>The IAM dashboard shows a summary of the security recommendations and IAM resources in your account. </p><p>You can find other account information along with links to other tools and resources to help you manage your account.</p>":"<p>The IAM dashboard shows a summary of the security recommendations and IAM resources in your account. </p><p>You can find other account information along with links to other tools and resources to help you manage your account.</p>","<p>You can use IAM Roles Anywhere to authenticate your non AWS workloads with X.509 certificates. When workloads authenticate with IAM Roles Anywhere they can use the temporary security credentials provided by a role to access your AWS resources.</p>":"<p>You can use IAM Roles Anywhere to authenticate your non AWS workloads with X.509 certificates. When workloads authenticate with IAM Roles Anywhere they can use the temporary security credentials provided by a role to access your AWS resources.</p>","<p>You manage access in AWS by creating policies and attaching them to IAM identities (users, groups of users, or roles) or AWS resources. A policy is an object in AWS that, when associated with an identity or resource, defines their permissions.</p>":"<p>You manage access in AWS by creating policies and attaching them to IAM identities (users, groups of users, or roles) or AWS resources. A policy is an object in AWS that, when associated with an identity or resource, defines their permissions.</p>","| Arn like |":"| Arn like |","| Arn not like |":"| Arn not like |","| Bool |":"| Bool |","| IP Address |":"| IP Address |","| Not Ip Address |":"| Not Ip Address |","| Null |":"| Null |","| string like |":"| string like |","| string not like |":"| string not like |","0-9 and A-F (not case sensitive).":"0-9 and A-F (not case sensitive).","1 action":"1 action","1 day":"1 day","1 hour":"1 hour","1 inline policy added":"1 inline policy added","1 inline policy removed":"1 inline policy removed","1 policy added":"1 policy added","1 policy removed":"1 policy removed","1 unused action":"1 unused action","1 user added to this group.":"1 user added to this group.","1 user removed from this group.":"1 user removed from this group.","10 resources":"10 resources","12 hours":"12 hours","2 hours":"2 hours","20 resources":"20 resources","30 days":"30 days","365 days":"365 days","4 hours":"4 hours","5 days":"5 days","60 days":"60 days","7 days":"7 days","8 hours":"8 hours","90 days":"90 days","A group with this name already exists.":"A group with this name already exists.","A new user group will be created.":"A new user group will be created.","A number is required.":"A number is required.","A parameter you included isn't constructed correctly. Please adjust your input and try again.":"A parameter you included isn't constructed correctly. Please adjust your input and try again.","A policy called {{nameValue}} already exists. Duplicate names are not allowed.":"A policy called {{nameValue}} already exists. Duplicate names are not allowed.","A policy is an object in AWS that defines permissions.":"A policy is an object in AWS that defines permissions.","A provider with this name already exists.":"A provider with this name already exists.","A provider 