 IdP, create an IAM identity provider entity to establish a trust relationship between your AWS account and the IdP. IAM supports IdPs that are compatible with <openid>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</openid> or <samlLink>SAML 2.0 (Security Assertion Markup Language 2.0)</samlLink>.</p>",components:{openid:a("a",{target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",href:"http://openid.net/connect/"}),samlLink:a("a",{target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",href:"https://wiki.oasis-open.org/security"})}}),Ly=a(dl,{i18nKey:"<p>If you have employees that require access to AWS, don't share root user credentials with them. Instead, create individual IAM users within your account that correspond to users in your organization. IAM users are not separate accounts; they are users within the same account. Each user can have its own unique set of long-term credentials for access to the AWS Management Console. You can also create an individual access key for each user so that the user can make programmatic requests to work with resources in your account. You can grant different permissions to each IAM user.</p><p>Alternatively, you can <sso>use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication</sso>.</p>",components:{sso:a("a",{target:"_blank",rel:"noopener noreferrer",href:`${Hp.value}singlesignon/latest/userguide/what-is.html`})}}),ky=s(dl,{children:[a("p",{children:"A role is an IAM identity that you can create in your account that has specific permissions. An IAM role has some similarities to an IAM user. Roles and users are both AWS identities with permissions policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS. However, instead of being uniquely associated with one person, a role can be assumed by anyone who needs it. A role does not have standard long-term credentials such as a password or access keys associated with it. Instead, when you assume a role, it provides you with temporary security credentials for your role session."}),a("p",{children:"You can use roles to delegate access to users, applications, or services that don't normally have access to your AWS resources."})]}),My=a(dl,{children:a("p",{children:"You can use IAM Roles Anywhere to authenticate your non AWS workloads with X.509 certificates. When workloads authenticate with IAM Roles Anywhere they can use the temporary security credentials provided by a role to access your AWS resources."})}),Uy=a(dl,{children:a("p",{children:"Policies are JSON documents in AWS that let you specify who has access to AWS resources, and what actions they can perform on those resources. You can attach a policy to an identity or resource to define their permissions. AWS evaluates these policies when the IAM principal (user or role) makes a request. Permissions in the policies determine whether the request is allowed or denied."})}),xy=e=>{if("string"==typeof e&&Pn(e,"group"))return Ty(e);if("string"==typeof e&&Pn(e,"dashboard"))return(e=>({id:e,pageContent:{header:ll("IAM dashboard"),body:s(dl,{children:[a("p",{children:"The IAM dashboard shows a summary of the security recommendations and IAM resources in your account. "}),a("p",{children:"You can find other account information along with links to other tools and resources to help you manage your account."})]}),links:[]}}))(e);const t=ll("Learn more"),{header:n,body:i,link:r}=(e=>{switch(e){case"user.list.help":return{header:ll("Users"),body:Ly,link:{text:ll("IAM Users"),href:`${Hp.value}console/iam/users`}};case"role.list.help":return{header:ll("Roles"),body:ky,link:{text:ll("IAM roles"),href:`${Hp.value}console/iam/roles`}};case"role.rolesAnywhere.help":return{header:ll("IAM Roles Anywhere"),body:My,link:{text:ll("Provide access for non AWS workloads"),href:`${Hp.value}IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_common-scenarios_non-aws.html`}};case"policy.list.help":return{header:ll("Policies"),body:Uy,link:{text:ll("Policies and permissions in IAM"),href:`${Hp.value}console/iam/about-iam-policies`}};default:return{header:ll("IAM Identity Providers"),body:Oy,l